<Section id="main-section" title="E&M Formula Sheet">
        <p>
            This page contains all formulas and information that will be provided to you on tests and quizzes in your Physics class.
        </p>
        
</Section>
	
        
		
<SubSection id="dynamics" title="Dynamics: Motion, Forces, and Energy">
<p>
            Formulas from Physics 1 that will be useful this semester.
        </p>

</SubSection>
		
		
<SubSubSection id="kinematics" title="Motion">
<p>These equations describe the <b>one dimensional</b> motion of an object traveling with constant acceleration <InlineEquation equation="a" /> from initial position <InlineEquation equation="x_0" /> and having initial velocity <InlineEquation equation="v_0" />.</p>

<DisplayEquation equation="\\begin{aligned}&{\\text{Position }\ x \\text{ at time } t:}&& {x=x_0+v_0 t + \\frac{1}{2}at^2} \\\\&{\\text{Velocity }\ v \\text{ at time } t:}&& v=at \\\\&{\\text{Final velocity }\ v \\text{ after traveling} \\Delta x :}&& v^2={v_0}^2+2a\\Delta x \\end{aligned}" background="true" title="One-dimensional constant acceleration motion:"/>

<DisplayEquation equation="a_c=\\frac{v^2}{r}" title="Centripetal acceleration: Acceleration perpendicular to an object's velocity will cause it to travel in a circle of radius r:" background="True" />

<DisplayEquation equation="\\vec{p}=m\\vec{v}" title="An object's momentum is a vector that points in the same direction as its velocity:" background="True" />


</SubSubSection>




<SubSubSection id="forces" title="Forces"> 
<CalloutContainer slot="cards">
<CalloutCard title="Acceleration due to gravity (near the surface of Earth)">
			We use "standard gravity" for calculations:<br>
			g=9.81 m/s<sup>2</sup>
            </CalloutCard>
        </CalloutContainer>   
<DisplayEquation equation="\\sum\\vec{F}=m\\vec{a}" title="The total (vector sum) force acting on an object will cause its (vector) acceleration:" background="True" />
<DisplayEquation equation="\\begin{aligned}&{\\text{Forces along }\ x \\text{ cause acceleration along } x:}&& F_{1x}+F_{2x}+\\cdots=ma_x \\\\&{\\text{Forces along }\ y \\text{ cause acceleration along } y:}&& F_{1y}+F_{2y}+\\cdots=ma_y \\end{aligned}" background="true" title="Turning 2D vectors into 1D for solving"/>

<DisplayEquation equation="\\begin{aligned}&{\\text{Force of gravity (near Earth's surface)}}&& F_g=mg \\\\&{\\text{Spring force (stretched distance x from equilibrium)}}&& \\vec{F}_s=-k\\vec{x} \\end{aligned}" background="true" title="Macroscopic force models"/>
<br>
<DisplayEquation equation="\\begin{aligned}\\tau&=F_\\perp  r \\\\ &=F r_\\perp \\\\ &=Fr\\sin{\\theta} \\end{aligned}" background="true" title="A force applied perpendicular to a distance r from an object's rotation axis will create torque about that axis:"/>
</SubSubSection>


<SubSubSection id="energy" title="Energy"> 
<DisplayEquation equation="\\begin{aligned}W_F&=F_\\parallel  d \\\\ &=F d_\\parallel \\\\ &=Fd\\cos{\\theta} \\end{aligned}" background="true" title="Each force F does work (transfers energy) when an object is displaced distance d parallel to that force:"/>
<DisplayEquation equation="\\begin{aligned}P_F&=F_\\parallel  v \\\\ &=F v_\\parallel \\\\ &=Fv\\cos{\\theta} \\end{aligned}" background="true" title="Each force F pushing an object at velocity v parallel to the force has power (energy transfer rate):"/>
<br><br>
<DisplayEquation equation="K=\\frac{1}{2}mv^2" title="A moving object has kinetic energy:" background="True"/>
<DisplayEquation equation="W_{total}=\\Delta K" title="Total work on an object changes that object's kinetic energy:" background="True"/>
<br><br>
<DisplayEquation equation="E=K+U" title="Total mechanical energy of an object combines kinetic and potential energy:" background="True"/>
<DisplayEquation equation="\\begin{aligned}&{\\text{Work by a conservative force borrows from its Potential Energy:}}&& W_c=-\\Delta U \\\\&{\\text{Close to Earth, gravity stores energy in height:}}&& U_{g}=mgh \\\\&{\\text{Springs store energy in compression or expansion:}}&& U_s=\\frac{1}{2}kx^2 \\end{aligned}" background="true" title="Conservative forces can store energy as Potential Energy" />
<DisplayEquation equation="E_i+W_{nc}=E_f" title="Nonconservative work changes the mechanical energy of an object:" background="True"/>
</SubSubSection>



<SubSubSection id="bigG" title="Universal Gravitation">

<CalloutContainer slot="cards">
<CalloutCard title="Gravitational constant">
			G=6.67×10<sup>-11</sup> N m<sup>2</sup>/kg<sup>2</sup>
            </CalloutCard>
        </CalloutContainer>
		
<p>Two objects having masses m₁ and m₂ will exert a gravitational force on each other.</p>
<p>This force is always mutual and attractive (i.e., the masses pull toward each other).</p>

<DisplayEquation equation="|\\vec{F}_{1\\text{ on }2}|=G\\frac{m_1 m_2}{{r}^2}" title="Magnitude of gravitational force exerted on mass 2 by mass 1 when separated by distance r:" background="True" />

<DisplayEquation equation="U_G=-G\\frac{m_1 m_2}{{r}}" title="Gravitational potential energy stored between the two masses is:" background="True" />
<Warning title="Negative potential energy" id="GPE">
            The negative sign is not a mistake in the formula sheet and should not be left out of your calculations. As the two masses get closer to each other, gravitational potential energy must decrease. Using the negative sign is the only way to make this possible.
        </Warning>
</SubSubSection>


